      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You cannot fry.' Senya links 'you' directly with 'fry' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Мясо жёсткое, как подошва.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The meat is tough as a shoe sole.' Notice how 'meat' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'shoe sole'. Then Fedya responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мы на флоте сапоги ели.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'In the navy we ate boots.' Fedya links 'we' directly with 'for' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "И ремни тоже ели.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And we ate belts too.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'and' with 'were eating'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Ты врёшь про сапоги.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You are lying about the boots.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'you' with 'boots'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Не веришь мне?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You don't believe me?' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'not' with 'to me'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Давай поспорим на пять штук.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let's bet five grand.' A compact expression built around 'let's' and 'pieces/grand'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Съешь мой ремень за пять штук?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You'll eat my belt for five grand?' Senya links 'eat' directly with 'my' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну, съем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well, I'll eat it.' Notice how 'oh' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'to eat up'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Луи, разбей.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Louis, break it.' Senya combines 'Louis' with 'to break' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Louis responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
